{% comment %}
  Build Your Own Crochet Bouquet — an interactive Canvas bouquet composer.

  The governing boundary: Shopify defines what can be purchased, this section
  defines how those purchasable items are arranged. The merchant's collection is
  the only source of truth for what is buyable — there are no hard-coded
  products, prices or IDs anywhere in this feature.

  What this template does, and nothing more:

  1. Emits the collection as a normalised JSON catalog in a script tag. Every
     price is formatted here with `money`, so no currency is ever formatted in
     JavaScript.
  2. Renders the picker cards server-side, so the products are real, linked
     markup that still reads as a product list with JavaScript off.
  3. Renders the Canvas, the accessible bouquet list and the cart button as
     inert markup for assets/bouquet-builder.js to bring to life.

  The customer chooses which flowers and how many. The arrangement — position,
  scale, rotation, layering, spacing — is produced by BouquetLayoutEngine in
  assets/bouquet-builder.js and is never anyone's manual work.

  Category, role and visual behaviour come from product metafields (the
  namespace is a setting, `custom` by default) — never from the product title:

    <ns>.bouquet_category  single line text   flower | filler | leaf | wrapper | ribbon | charm
    <ns>.bouquet_role      single line text   focal | secondary | filler | greenery | accent
    <ns>.bouquet_visual    file reference     the drawing of this piece
    <ns>.bouquet_scale     number decimal     optional
    <ns>.bouquet_layer     number integer     optional

  Every one of them is optional. Without a role, one is inferred from the
  category; without a category the product joins a single "All" group; without
  a drawing the builder renders a simple shape from the category. A missing
  metafield must never break the builder.

  `role` is what the composition engine actually reasons about, and it decides
  two things: where a piece sits in the arrangement, and whether it counts
  towards the bouquet at all. Anything with role `accent` is an accessory — a
  wrap, a ribbon, a charm — and is deliberately excluded from the flower count.

  What bouquet_visual should hold:

    - SVG for preference: line art scales crisply at any Canvas size and weighs
      almost nothing. Transparent PNG or WebP around 600 x 900 also work.
    - Portrait, stem running down to the bottom edge, bloom at the top.
    - Trimmed tight to the drawing. The art is drawn whole and uncropped, so any
      surrounding padding becomes a gap in the bouquet.
    - Drawn to a consistent scale across the set. Use bouquet_scale for the
      exceptions rather than redrawing.

  The product photo is never drawn on the Canvas — a photograph carries its own
  background and its own light, and a dozen layered together read as a collage.
  It stays on the picker card where it belongs.

  Reused from the theme: the colour-scheme system, `.store-width` for the page
  gutter, Dawn's `.button` / `.field` recipes, `assets/pubsub.js` for the cart
  handoff and whichever of <cart-drawer> / <cart-notification> the merchant has
  configured. No cart logic is reimplemented.

  The 50-product ceiling: `collection.products` returns at most 50 products in
  Liquid. That is documented in the collection setting's info text, and a
  merchant who exceeds it sees a notice in the Theme Editor rather than silently
  losing products.
{% endcomment %}

{%- comment -%}
  Components first, this section's own stylesheet last — the same order
  sections/product-showcase.liquid uses, and it matters.

  The picker cards carry Dawn's `.slider__slide`, which sets `padding-bottom: 0`.
  That is the same specificity as `.bouquet-card`, so whichever file loads later
  wins: with the section stylesheet first, Dawn silently flattened the bottom
  padding off every card and no amount of adjusting the card's own rules could
  bring it back.

  `.store-width` is here for a different reason: it is only pulled in by the
  sections that use it, so without this line the container would have no rules
  at all on a page with no collection section, and the builder would run edge to
  edge.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'component-store-width.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'component-slider.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'section-bouquet-builder.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
<script src="{{ 'bouquet-builder.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>

{%- liquid
  assign ns = section.settings.metafield_namespace | strip | default: 'custom'
  assign bouquet_collection = section.settings.collection
  assign products = bouquet_collection.products
  assign product_count = products | size
  assign total_in_collection = bouquet_collection.all_products_count | default: product_count

  comment
    A piece with no photograph has nothing to draw on the Canvas, so it is held
    back rather than shown as a gap the customer can add. Nothing needs
    switching on later: the moment a photograph lands on the product, the piece
    appears in the picker of its own accord.

    The merchant can turn the gate off to see everything.
  endcomment
  assign require_photo = section.settings.require_photo

  comment
    The categories in use, the ones with nothing to show, and how many pieces
    are actually pickable — all decided in one pass. Never a hard-coded list.
  endcomment
  assign category_keys = ''
  assign shown_count = 0
  assign uncategorised_count = 0

  for product in products
    assign category = product.metafields[ns]['bouquet_category'].value | strip

    if require_photo and product.featured_media == blank
      continue
    endif

    assign shown_count = shown_count | plus: 1
    if category == blank
      assign uncategorised_count = uncategorised_count | plus: 1
    else
      assign category_keys = category_keys | append: category | append: '|'
    endif
  endfor

  assign categories = category_keys | split: '|' | uniq | sort
  assign category_count = categories | size
-%}

{%- if bouquet_collection == blank -%}
  {%- if request.design_mode -%}
    <div class="bouquet bouquet--notice color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }} gradient">
      <div class="bouquet__inner store-width">
        <p class="bouquet__notice">
          Choose a bouquet collection in this section's settings. Its products become the flowers,
          leaves and wrappers customers can build with.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- elsif shown_count == 0 -%}
  {%- if request.design_mode -%}
    <div class="bouquet bouquet--notice color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }} gradient">
      <div class="bouquet__inner store-width">
        <p class="bouquet__notice">
          <strong>{{ bouquet_collection.title }}</strong> has no products yet, so there is nothing to
          build a bouquet from. Add products to that collection and this section will fill itself in.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- style -%}
    #BouquetBuilder-{{ section.id }} {
      /*
        The measure every other section on this store uses. `.store-width`
        defaults to 180rem, which is only 1800px while the merchant's body
        scale is exactly 1 — stating it in px keeps this section lined up with
        the rest of the page whatever that setting is.
      */
      --store-width: {{ section.settings.content_width }}px;
      --bouquet-padding-top: {{ section.settings.padding_top | times: 0.75 | round: 0 }}px;
      --bouquet-padding-bottom: {{ section.settings.padding_bottom | times: 0.75 | round: 0 }}px;
      --bouquet-stage-ratio: {{ section.settings.canvas_ratio }};
      --bouquet-radius: {{ section.settings.corner_radius }}px;
    }

    @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
      #BouquetBuilder-{{ section.id }} {
        --bouquet-padding-top: {{ section.settings.padding_top }}px;
        --bouquet-padding-bottom: {{ section.settings.padding_bottom }}px;
      }
    }
  {%- endstyle -%}

  <div id="BouquetBuilder-{{ section.id }}" class="bouquet color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }} gradient">
    <bouquet-builder
      class="bouquet__inner store-width"
      data-section-id="{{ section.id }}"
      data-min-flowers="{{ section.settings.min_flowers }}"
      data-max-accessories="{{ section.settings.max_accessories }}"
      data-max-per-item="{{ section.settings.max_per_item }}"
      data-enable-drag="{{ section.settings.enable_drag }}"
      data-enable-rotate="{{ section.settings.enable_rotate }}"
      data-enable-scale="{{ section.settings.enable_scale }}"
      {% if section.settings.background_image != blank %}
        data-background="{{ section.settings.background_image | image_url: width: 1600 }}"
      {% endif %}
    >
      {%- capture catalog_json -%}
        {
          "moneyFormat": {{ shop.money_format | json }},
          "items": [
            {%- assign first_entry = true -%}
            {%- for product in products -%}
              {%- liquid
                assign category = product.metafields[ns]['bouquet_category'].value | strip
                assign role = product.metafields[ns]['bouquet_role'].value | strip
                assign visual_scale = product.metafields[ns]['bouquet_scale'].value
                assign visual_layer = product.metafields[ns]['bouquet_layer'].value
              -%}
              {%- for variant in product.variants -%}
                {%- liquid
                  comment
                    The variant's own photograph, falling back to the product's.
                    This is what the Canvas draws — the actual product, at a size
                    worth zooming into, not a drawing of it. A variant with no
                    photograph draws nothing on the Canvas, and the picker card
                    says so.
                  endcomment
                  assign card_image = variant.featured_media.preview_image | default: product.featured_media.preview_image
                  assign thumb_url = ''
                  assign canvas_url = ''
                  if card_image != blank
                    assign thumb_url = card_image | image_url: width: 400
                    assign canvas_url = card_image | image_url: width: 900
                  endif

                  comment
                    A wrap is drawn in two halves. The variant's own picture is
                    the backing sheet; the front flap is the product image whose
                    alt names this variant and says 'front'. Matched on alt
                    rather than on position, because the two are the same size
                    and colour and Shopify does not promise an order — a
                    position rule here would swap them at random.
                  endcomment
                  assign front_url = ''
                  assign front_alt = product.title | append: ' — ' | append: variant.title | append: ' (front)'
                  for media in product.media
                    if media.alt == front_alt
                      assign front_url = media.preview_image | image_url: width: 900
                      break
                    endif
                  endfor
                -%}
                {%- unless first_entry %},{% endunless -%}
                {%- assign first_entry = false -%}
                {
                  "id": {{ variant.id | prepend: 'variant-' | json }},
                  "variantId": {{ variant.id }},
                  "productId": {{ product.id }},
                  "title": {{ product.title | json }},
                  "variantTitle": {% if product.has_only_default_variant %}""{% else %}{{ variant.title | json }}{% endif %},
                  "price": {{ variant.price }},
                  "priceFormatted": {{ variant.price | money | strip_html | json }},
                  "available": {{ variant.available | json }},
                  "url": {{ variant.url | default: product.url | json }},
                  "image": {{ thumb_url | json }},
                  "category": {{ category | default: '' | json }},
                  "role": {{ role | default: '' | json }},
                  "visual": {
                    "asset": {{ canvas_url | default: '' | json }},
                    "front": {{ front_url | default: '' | json }},
                    "scale": {% if visual_scale != blank %}{{ visual_scale }}{% else %}null{% endif %},
                    "layer": {% if visual_layer != blank %}{{ visual_layer | round: 0 }}{% else %}null{% endif %}
                  }
                }
              {%- endfor -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          ]
        }
      {%- endcapture -%}
      <script type="application/json" data-bouquet-catalog>{{ catalog_json }}</script>

      {%- if section.settings.heading != blank or section.settings.description != blank -%}
        <header class="bouquet__header">
          {%- if section.settings.heading != blank -%}
            <h2 class="bouquet__heading">{{ section.settings.heading }}</h2>
          {%- endif -%}
          {%- if section.settings.description != blank -%}
            <div class="bouquet__description rte">{{ section.settings.description }}</div>
          {%- endif -%}
        </header>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if request.design_mode and total_in_collection > 50 -%}
        <p class="bouquet__notice bouquet__notice--inline">
          <strong>{{ bouquet_collection.title }}</strong> holds {{ total_in_collection }} products, and
          a theme can only read the first 50 of a collection. The
          {{ total_in_collection | minus: 50 }} beyond that will not appear in the builder.
        </p>
      {%- endif -%}

      <div class="bouquet__layout">
        <div class="bouquet__stage">
          <div class="bouquet__canvas-wrap" data-bouquet-stage>
            <canvas
              class="bouquet__canvas"
              data-bouquet-canvas
              role="img"
              aria-label="A preview of the bouquet you are building. The list of items below describes what it contains, and can be changed there."
            ></canvas>

            {%- comment -%}
              The controls sit on the Canvas, at the top, because that is what
              they act on — reaching away to a bar underneath to send a flower
              behind another breaks the loop between doing and seeing.

              The bar itself takes no pointer events, only its buttons do, so
              the Canvas underneath stays fully draggable right up to the edge.

              Depth needs real buttons: the Canvas offers no way to reach behind
              a flower to the one underneath, and by keyboard there is no way at
              all. Those two are disabled until something is selected, so they
              never look available when they would do nothing.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <div class="bouquet__toolbar">
              <div class="bouquet__toolgroup bouquet__toolgroup--layers">
                <button
                  class="bouquet__tool"
                  type="button"
                  data-bouquet-backward
                  title="Send the selected flower behind the one in front of it"
                  disabled
                >
                  <span class="bouquet__icon bouquet__icon--back" aria-hidden="true">
                    {{- 'icon-caret.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}
                  </span>
                  <span class="visually-hidden">Send the selected flower one layer towards the back</span>
                </button>
                <button
                  class="bouquet__tool"
                  type="button"
                  data-bouquet-forward
                  title="Bring the selected flower in front of the one behind it"
                  disabled
                >
                  <span class="bouquet__icon bouquet__icon--front" aria-hidden="true">
                    {{- 'icon-caret.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}
                  </span>
                  <span class="visually-hidden">Bring the selected flower one layer towards the front</span>
                </button>
              </div>

              <div class="bouquet__toolgroup">
                {%- comment -%}
                  Off by default. Re-arranging is the one tool that moves a
                  bouquet somebody has already placed by hand, so it stays
                  behind a switch until it is wanted rather than being a button
                  people press once and regret.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- if section.settings.enable_shuffle -%}
                  <button
                    class="bouquet__tool"
                    type="button"
                    data-bouquet-shuffle
                    title="Re-arrange the same flowers"
                    disabled
                  >
                    <span class="bouquet__icon" aria-hidden="true">
                      {{- 'icon-shuffle.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}
                    </span>
                    <span class="visually-hidden">
                      {{- section.settings.shuffle_label | default: 'Shuffle' -}}
                    </span>
                  </button>
                {%- endif -%}
                <button class="bouquet__tool" type="button" data-bouquet-undo title="Undo the last change" disabled>
                  <span class="bouquet__icon" aria-hidden="true">
                    {{- 'icon-undo.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}
                  </span>
                  <span class="visually-hidden">Undo the last change</span>
                </button>
                <button class="bouquet__tool" type="button" data-bouquet-reset title="Empty the bouquet and start again" disabled>
                  <span class="bouquet__icon" aria-hidden="true">{{- 'icon-remove.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}</span>
                  <span class="visually-hidden">Empty the bouquet and start again</span>
                </button>
              </div>
            </div>

          </div>

          {%- comment -%}
            Progress, not validation. The customer is told how close the bouquet
            is to being complete and then that it is complete — never that they
            have made an error. Everything here is written by the script; the
            markup only reserves the space and the wording of the empty state.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="bouquet__progress">
            <div class="bouquet__progress-text">
              <p class="bouquet__progress-headline" data-bouquet-progress-headline></p>
              <p class="bouquet__progress-meta">
                <span class="bouquet__progress-count" data-bouquet-progress-count hidden></span>
                <span class="bouquet__progress-total" data-bouquet-progress-total hidden></span>
              </p>
              <p class="bouquet__progress-detail" data-bouquet-progress-detail hidden></p>
            </div>

            <div class="bouquet__progress-bar" data-bouquet-progress-bar hidden aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="bouquet__progress-fill"></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        {%- comment -%}
          The picker: a kind is chosen from the tabs, and its products scroll
          horizontally in one row.

          Every row is rendered, and with JavaScript off they all simply show —
          so the products stay readable as a plain list. The script then hides
          all but the chosen one, which is what keeps the section short: six
          stacked rows made the panel twice the height of the bouquet beside it
          and left the customer scrolling away from the very thing they were
          building.

          The tabs are the categories the merchant actually used — never a
          hard-coded list. Products with no category at all get one tab of their
          own so nothing can go missing.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="bouquet__panel">
          {%- if shown_count > 8 -%}
            <div class="bouquet__search field">
              <input
                class="field__input"
                type="search"
                id="BouquetSearch-{{ section.id }}"
                data-bouquet-search
                placeholder=" "
                autocomplete="off"
              >
              <label class="field__label" for="BouquetSearch-{{ section.id }}">Search the flowers</label>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            The kind being browsed. These are plain toggle buttons rather than a
            full ARIA tab widget: they filter what is on show, they are reachable
            with Tab and Enter like any button, and nothing here needs the arrow
            key contract a real tablist would owe the customer.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if category_count > 1 or uncategorised_count > 0 -%}
            <div class="bouquet__tabs" role="group" aria-label="Choose what to browse">
              {%- assign tab_index = 0 -%}
              {%- for category in categories -%}
                {%- liquid
                  case category
                    when 'flower'
                      assign tab_title = 'Flowers'
                    when 'filler'
                      assign tab_title = 'Fillers'
                    when 'leaf'
                      assign tab_title = 'Greenery'
                    when 'wrapper'
                      assign tab_title = 'Wraps'
                    when 'ribbon'
                      assign tab_title = 'Ribbons'
                    when 'charm'
                      assign tab_title = 'Charms'
                    else
                      assign tab_title = category | replace: '-', ' ' | replace: '_', ' ' | capitalize
                  endcase
                -%}
                <button
                  class="bouquet__tab"
                  type="button"
                  data-bouquet-tab="{{ category | escape }}"
                  aria-pressed="{% if tab_index == 0 %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
                >
                  {{ tab_title }}
                </button>
                {%- assign tab_index = tab_index | plus: 1 -%}
              {%- endfor -%}

              {%- if uncategorised_count > 0 -%}
                <button
                  class="bouquet__tab"
                  type="button"
                  data-bouquet-tab=""
                  aria-pressed="{% if tab_index == 0 %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
                >
                  {%- if category_count > 0 -%}More{%- else -%}Everything{%- endif -%}
                </button>
              {%- endif -%}
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}

              {%- comment -%}
            Each kind is a real slider, not a bare scroll box: Dawn's own
            <slider-component> from assets/global.js, exactly as
            sections/product-showcase.liquid uses it. It brings arrows that
            disable themselves at either end, page maths that re-runs on resize,
            smooth stepped scrolling and scroll-snap — so no slider code is
            written here and no library is added.

            The ids matter: <slider-component> finds its track by `Slider-` and
            its slides by `Slide-`, so both are namespaced by section and row to
            keep several sliders on one page independent.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- for category in categories -%}
            {%- liquid
              case category
                when 'flower'
                  assign row_title = 'Flowers'
                when 'filler'
                  assign row_title = 'Fillers'
                when 'leaf'
                  assign row_title = 'Greenery'
                when 'wrapper'
                  assign row_title = 'Wraps'
                when 'ribbon'
                  assign row_title = 'Ribbons'
                when 'charm'
                  assign row_title = 'Charms'
                else
                  assign row_title = category | replace: '-', ' ' | replace: '_', ' ' | capitalize
              endcase
              assign slider_id = 'Slider-' | append: section.id | append: '-' | append: forloop.index
            -%}
            <section class="bouquet-row" data-bouquet-row="{{ category | escape }}">
              <h3 class="bouquet-row__title">{{ row_title }}</h3>

              <slider-component class="bouquet-row__slider">
                <ul class="bouquet-row__track slider slider--everywhere list-unstyled" id="{{ slider_id }}" role="list">
                  {%- assign slide_index = 0 -%}
                  {%- for product in products -%}
                    {%- if require_photo and product.featured_media == blank -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
                    {%- assign product_category = product.metafields[ns]['bouquet_category'].value | strip -%}
                    {%- if product_category == category -%}
                      {%- for variant in product.variants -%}
                        {%- assign slide_index = slide_index | plus: 1 -%}
                        {%- comment -%}
                          Derived from the row's own slider id, so slides can never
                          collide across rows. forloop.parentloop.index here is
                          the product's position, not the row's, and two rows
                          holding a third product would have produced the same id.
                        {%- endcomment -%}
                        {%- capture slide_id -%}{{ slider_id | replace: 'Slider-', 'Slide-' }}-{{ slide_index }}{%- endcapture -%}
                        {%- render 'bouquet-card',
                          product: product,
                          variant: variant,
                          category: category,
                          slide_id: slide_id
                        -%}
                      {%- endfor -%}
                    {%- endif -%}
                  {%- endfor -%}
                </ul>

                <div class="bouquet-row__controls">
                  <button
                    class="bouquet-row__arrow bouquet-row__arrow--prev"
                    type="button"
                    name="previous"
                    aria-label="{{ 'general.slider.previous_slide' | t }}"
                    aria-controls="{{ slider_id }}"
                  >
                    <span class="svg-wrapper" aria-hidden="true">{{- 'icon-arrow.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}</span>
                  </button>
                  <button
                    class="bouquet-row__arrow bouquet-row__arrow--next"
                    type="button"
                    name="next"
                    aria-label="{{ 'general.slider.next_slide' | t }}"
                    aria-controls="{{ slider_id }}"
                  >
                    <span class="svg-wrapper" aria-hidden="true">{{- 'icon-arrow.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}</span>
                  </button>
                </div>
              </slider-component>
            </section>
          {%- endfor -%}

          {%- if uncategorised_count > 0 -%}
            {%- assign loose_slider_id = 'Slider-' | append: section.id | append: '-loose' -%}
            <section class="bouquet-row" data-bouquet-row="">
              <h3 class="bouquet-row__title">
                {%- if category_count > 0 -%}More to add{%- else -%}Everything{%- endif -%}
              </h3>

              <slider-component class="bouquet-row__slider">
                <ul
                  class="bouquet-row__track slider slider--everywhere list-unstyled"
                  id="{{ loose_slider_id }}"
                  role="list"
                >
                  {%- assign slide_index = 0 -%}
                  {%- for product in products -%}
                    {%- if require_photo and product.featured_media == blank -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
                    {%- assign product_category = product.metafields[ns]['bouquet_category'].value | strip -%}
                    {%- if product_category == blank -%}
                      {%- for variant in product.variants -%}
                        {%- assign slide_index = slide_index | plus: 1 -%}
                        {%- capture slide_id -%}{{ loose_slider_id | replace: 'Slider-', 'Slide-' }}-{{ slide_index }}{%- endcapture -%}
                        {%- render 'bouquet-card',
                          product: product,
                          variant: variant,
                          category: '',
                          slide_id: slide_id
                        -%}
                      {%- endfor -%}
                    {%- endif -%}
                  {%- endfor -%}
                </ul>

                <div class="bouquet-row__controls">
                  <button
                    class="bouquet-row__arrow bouquet-row__arrow--prev"
                    type="button"
                    name="previous"
                    aria-label="{{ 'general.slider.previous_slide' | t }}"
                    aria-controls="{{ loose_slider_id }}"
                  >
                    <span class="svg-wrapper" aria-hidden="true">{{- 'icon-arrow.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}</span>
                  </button>
                  <button
                    class="bouquet-row__arrow bouquet-row__arrow--next"
                    type="button"
                    name="next"
                    aria-label="{{ 'general.slider.next_slide' | t }}"
                    aria-controls="{{ loose_slider_id }}"
                  >
                    <span class="svg-wrapper" aria-hidden="true">{{- 'icon-arrow.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}</span>
                  </button>
                </div>
              </slider-component>
            </section>
          {%- endif -%}

          <p class="bouquet__no-results" data-bouquet-no-results hidden>
            Nothing here matches that. Try another word.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bouquet__summary" data-bouquet-summary>
          <div class="bouquet__summary-head">
            <h3 class="bouquet__summary-title">Your bouquet</h3>
          </div>

          {%- comment -%}
            The bouquet's contents as compact chips rather than a list of rows.
            A row per item grows into a wall of text as the bouquet fills, which
            is the opposite of what a summary is for. Each chip is still a real
            button, so removing a piece without the Canvas stays possible for
            anyone using a keyboard or a screen reader — and the quantity
            steppers live on the picker cards, where the choosing happens.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <ul class="bouquet__lines list-unstyled" data-bouquet-lines role="list"></ul>

          <div class="bouquet__summary-bar">
            <div class="bouquet__total">
              <span class="bouquet__total-label">Total</span>
              <span class="bouquet__total-value" data-bouquet-total>{{ 0 | money }}</span>
            </div>

            {%- comment -%}
              The same `.button` + `.loading__spinner` pairing Dawn's own buy
              button uses, so the busy state is the theme's, not a second one.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <button class="button bouquet__submit" type="button" data-bouquet-submit disabled>
              <span>{{ section.settings.button_label | default: 'Add bouquet to cart' }}</span>
              {%- render 'loading-spinner' -%}
            </button>
          </div>

          <p class="bouquet__message" data-bouquet-message role="status"></p>
          <p class="bouquet__error" data-bouquet-error role="alert" hidden></p>
          <p class="bouquet__disclaimer">Prices and totals are confirmed at checkout.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </bouquet-builder>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Bouquet builder",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "collection",
      "id": "collection",
      "label": "Bouquet collection",
      "info": "The flowers, leaves, fillers and wrappers customers can build with. A theme can only read the first 50 products of a collection — keep this one at or below 50."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "metafield_namespace",
      "label": "Metafield namespace",
      "default": "custom",
      "info": "Where bouquet_category, bouquet_visual, bouquet_scale and bouquet_layer live on the product. All four are optional."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "require_photo",
      "label": "Only offer pieces that have a photograph",
      "info": "A piece with no photograph has nothing to draw on the canvas. Leave this on and each one appears by itself once its photograph is added.",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Text"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "Heading",
      "default": "Build your own bouquet"
    },
    {
      "type": "richtext",
      "id": "description",
      "label": "Description",
      "default": "<p>Pick your flowers one stem at a time and watch them gather into a bouquet made for the person you have in mind.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enable_shuffle",
      "label": "Show the shuffle button",
      "info": "Re-arranges the same flowers into a new composition. Off until the arrangements are worth re-rolling.",
      "default": false
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "shuffle_label",
      "label": "Shuffle button label",
      "default": "Shuffle bouquet"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "button_label",
      "label": "Cart button label",
      "default": "Add bouquet to cart"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Rules"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "min_flowers",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 20,
      "step": 1,
      "label": "Flowers needed to complete a bouquet",
      "info": "The bouquet can be added to the cart once it holds this many. Accessories are not counted.",
      "default": 6
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "max_accessories",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 10,
      "step": 1,
      "label": "Most accessories a bouquet can hold",
      "info": "Wraps, ribbons and charms. Counted separately from flowers.",
      "default": 4
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "max_per_item",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 20,
      "step": 1,
      "label": "Most of any one piece",
      "default": 6
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Canvas"
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "background_image",
      "label": "Canvas background",
      "info": "The wrapping paper or surface the bouquet is arranged on."
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "canvas_ratio",
      "min": 0.6,
      "max": 1.4,
      "step": 0.1,
      "label": "Canvas shape",
      "info": "Width divided by height. Below 1 is portrait.",
      "default": 0.9
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enable_drag",
      "label": "Let customers move flowers",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enable_rotate",
      "label": "Let customers turn flowers",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enable_scale",
      "label": "Let customers resize flowers",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Appearance"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "content_width",
      "min": 1000,
      "max": 2000,
      "step": 20,
      "unit": "px",
      "label": "Content width",
      "default": 1800
    },
    {
      "type": "color_scheme",
      "id": "color_scheme",
      "label": "t:sections.all.colors.label",
      "default": "scheme-1"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "corner_radius",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 40,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "label": "Corner radius",
      "default": 20
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:sections.all.padding.section_padding_heading"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding_top",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "label": "t:sections.all.padding.padding_top",
      "default": 56
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding_bottom",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "label": "t:sections.all.padding.padding_bottom",
      "default": 56
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Bouquet builder"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
